/// A String is a collection of Char and answers the members that read it.
///
/// Issue #42.  ALGOL-24.md states it under 'A String is a collection of Char':
/// a String is a collection like any other, so Length and IsEmpty are the
/// properties every collection has, and Contains, IndexOf, Get and ToList are
/// the methods that read without modifying.
///
/// Length (S) is unaffected and stays the portable spelling.  This rule says
/// what '.' reaches on a String; it does not restrict what a function may be
/// applied to, and the function form is asserted here alongside so a fix that
/// broke it would show.
///
/// ⚠️ The two halves fail at DIFFERENT assertions, which is the useful part.
/// Interpreted, the first one raises: 'Only instances have properties.'
/// rejects the whole dotted form on a String, so S.Length and S.Anything fail
/// alike.  Compiled, the first two PASS -- alg_property answers Length and
/// IsEmpty by falling past every is_obj check -- and the third raises 'Only a
/// collection has ''Contains''.'  So the compiled half is two members short of
/// the rule, not five, and a fix there is smaller than a fix in the
/// interpreter.
///
/// ⚠️ ASCII only, deliberately.  If a String is a collection of Char then
/// S.Length counts CODE POINTS, and today it counts bytes -- issue #6.  Using
/// only ASCII keeps this file testing one thing: whether the members are
/// reachable at all.  The unit is #6's to fix, and ⚠️ the two must land
/// together, because S.Length, Length (S) and 'for C in S' have to agree.
///
/// ⚠️ ToList is asserted by rendering rather than element by element.  A List
/// of Char renders as '[h, e, l, l, o]', which pins the element type as well
/// as the contents -- a ToList that answered a List of one-character Strings
/// would render identically, but Copy (S, 0, 1) is a String and S[0] is a
/// Char, and the specification says the elements are Char.
///
/// Interpreted: fails at the first assertion.
/// Compiled:    fails at the third.
///
test 'A String Answers The Read-Only Collection Members';
begin
    var S : String := 'hello';

    AssertEqual (5, S.Length);
    AssertEqual (5, Length (S));

    AssertTrue (not S.IsEmpty);

    AssertTrue (S.Contains ('e'));
    AssertTrue (not S.Contains ('z'));

    AssertEqual (2, S.IndexOf ('l'));
    AssertEqual (-1, S.IndexOf ('z'));

    AssertEqual ('h', S.Get (0));

    AssertEqual ('[h, e, l, l, o]', Str (S.ToList ()));
end

/// The empty String answers too, and IsEmpty is the property that says so.
///
/// ⚠️ Separate test block, not a line in the one above.  The block above stops
/// at its first failing assertion, so a fix that reached Length but not
/// IsEmpty would leave this case unreported.
///
test 'The Empty String Answers IsEmpty';
begin
    var S : String := '';

    AssertTrue (S.IsEmpty);
    AssertEqual (0, S.Length);
end
